Input: {"question": "Who is Regina M. Anderson's spouse?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 0, "end": 12, "text": "As Regina M."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 13, "end": 159, "text": "Anderson sat at her desk, surrounded by stacks of dusty tomes and scribbled notes, she couldn't help but feel a sense of pride and accomplishment."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 160, "end": 275, "text": "Her latest play, \"The Whispering Walls,\" was finally taking shape, and she knew it was going to be her masterpiece."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 276, "end": 375, "text": "The words flowed effortlessly onto the page, as if the characters themselves were guiding her hand."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 376, "end": 465, "text": "Just as she was about to write the final act, a gentle knock at the door broke the spell."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 466, "end": 588, "text": "She looked up to see her husband, John Fullerton of that Ilk, standing in the doorway with a mischievous glint in his eye."}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 589, "end": 713, "text": "His tweed jacket was rumpled, and his tie was loosened, giving him a rakish air that always made Regina's heart skip a beat."}, "D1S8": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 714, "end": 804, "text": "\"Darling, I've brought some inspiration,\" he said, holding up a small, leather-bound book."}, "D1S9": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 805, "end": 846, "text": "\"I found this in the attic of our estate."}, "D1S10": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 847, "end": 898, "text": "It's an old diary belonging to one of my ancestors."}, "D1S11": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 899, "end": 935, "text": "I think you'll find it fascinating.\""}, "D1S12": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 936, "end": 987, "text": "Regina's eyes lit up as she took the book from him."}, "D1S13": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 988, "end": 1038, "text": "\"Oh, John, you always know just what to bring me.\""}, "D1S14": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1039, "end": 1137, "text": "She opened the book, and a yellowed page fell open, revealing a passage written in elegant script."}, "D1S15": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1138, "end": 1186, "text": "As she read, Regina's imagination began to soar."}, "D1S16": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1187, "end": 1322, "text": "She could see the characters from her play coming to life, their stories intertwining with the secrets hidden within the diary's pages."}, "D1S17": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1323, "end": 1447, "text": "John sat down beside her, his eyes scanning the words as she read aloud, his fingers absently stroking the back of her hand."}, "D1S18": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1448, "end": 1575, "text": "Together, they delved into the world of the diary, uncovering tales of love, loss, and family secrets that spanned generations."}, "D1S19": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1576, "end": 1709, "text": "The words on the page seemed to whisper secrets to Regina, and she knew that this was the key to unlocking the final act of her play."}, "D1S20": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1710, "end": 1824, "text": "As the sun dipped below the horizon, casting the room in a warm, golden light, Regina turned to John with a smile."}, "D1S21": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1825, "end": 1891, "text": "\"I think I have it,\" she said, her eyes sparkling with excitement."}, "D1S22": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1892, "end": 1947, "text": "\"The ending, the characters, it's all coming together.\""}, "D1S23": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1948, "end": 2017, "text": "John's face lit up with pride, and he leaned in to kiss her forehead."}, "D1S24": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2018, "end": 2057, "text": "\"I knew you'd do it, my love,\" he said."}, "D1S25": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2058, "end": 2085, "text": "\"You always were a genius.\""}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 0, "end": 9, "text": "Regina M."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 10, "end": 18, "text": "Anderson"}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 20, "end": 29, "text": "Regina M."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 30, "end": 116, "text": "Anderson (1900-1993) was an American playwright and librarian of multi-racial descent."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 117, "end": 234, "text": "Born in Chicago, Illinois, Anderson was a prominent figure in the Harlem Renaissance movement of the 1920s and 1930s."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 235, "end": 256, "text": "Early Life and Career"}, "D2S7": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 258, "end": 376, "text": "Anderson was born to a family of mixed racial heritage, with African American, Native American, and European ancestry."}, "D2S8": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 377, "end": 463, "text": "She grew up in a culturally rich environment, which would later influence her writing."}, "D2S9": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 464, "end": 663, "text": "Anderson pursued a career in librarianship, working at the 135th Street Branch of the New York Public Library, a hub for African American literary and cultural activity during the Harlem Renaissance."}, "D2S10": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 664, "end": 679, "text": "Literary Career"}, "D2S11": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 681, "end": 832, "text": "Anderson's playwriting career spanned several decades, during which she wrote several plays that explored themes of race, identity, and social justice."}, "D2S12": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 833, "end": 950, "text": "Her work was heavily influenced by her experiences as a woman of color and her involvement in the Harlem Renaissance."}, "D2S13": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 951, "end": 1067, "text": "Anderson's plays were performed at various venues, including the Harlem Experimental Theatre and the Krigwa Players."}, "D2S14": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1068, "end": 1081, "text": "Personal Life"}, "D2S15": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1083, "end": 1092, "text": "Regina M."}, "D2S16": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1093, "end": 1127, "text": "Anderson was married to William T."}, "D2S17": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1128, "end": 1174, "text": "Andrews, an American politician from New York."}, "D2S18": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1175, "end": 1327, "text": "Andrews served as a member of the New York State Assembly from 1933 to 1941 and was a prominent figure in New York politics during the mid-20th century."}, "D2S19": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1328, "end": 1348, "text": "Evidence of Marriage"}, "D2S20": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1350, "end": 1483, "text": "According to various sources, including the New York Amsterdam News and the Schomburg Center for Research in Black Culture, Regina M."}, "D2S21": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1484, "end": 1518, "text": "Anderson was married to William T."}, "D2S22": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1519, "end": 1527, "text": "Andrews."}, "D2S23": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1528, "end": 1633, "text": "A 1935 article in the New York Amsterdam News mentions Anderson's marriage to Andrews, stating that \"Mrs."}, "D2S24": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1634, "end": 1644, "text": "William T."}, "D2S25": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1645, "end": 1727, "text": "Andrews, playwright and librarian, is one of the most talented women of the race.\""}, "D2S26": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1728, "end": 1949, "text": "(1) The Schomburg Center for Research in Black Culture, a leading repository of African American cultural and historical materials, also confirms Anderson's marriage to Andrews in its biographical entry on the playwright."}, "D2S27": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1950, "end": 1953, "text": "(2)"}, "D2S28": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1955, "end": 1966, "text": "References:"}, "D2S29": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1968, "end": 2023, "text": "(1) \"Society\" (New York Amsterdam News, March 16, 1935)"}, "D2S30": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2025, "end": 2039, "text": "(2) \"Regina M."}, "D2S31": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2040, "end": 2123, "text": "Anderson\" (Schomburg Center for Research in Black Culture, New York Public Library)"}, "D2S32": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2125, "end": 2135, "text": "William T."}, "D2S33": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2136, "end": 2143, "text": "Andrews"}, "D2S34": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2145, "end": 2155, "text": "William T."}, "D2S35": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2156, "end": 2217, "text": "Andrews (1898-1984) was an American politician from New York."}, "D2S36": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2218, "end": 2365, "text": "He served as a member of the New York State Assembly from 1933 to 1941 and was a prominent figure in New York politics during the mid-20th century."}}, "candidates": [{"candidate_id": "C0", "answer": "John Fullerton of that Ilk", "card_ids": ["d1:c0"]}], "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "d1", "card_id": "d1:c0", "entity": 